417] [17592186532662 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532662 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({y}^{2}+6y+{3}^{2})+2=(${{field-1}}$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532662 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "68e816d0-08a0-579d-bfa8-21cac7d54b69" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532662 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 3y+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+3)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+6y+{3}^{2})+2 & = \\\\ (y+3)^{2}+2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250468" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-8y+26.$\n\n${y}^{2}-8y+26=(${{field-1}}$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7bec21b0-2997-5975-9f9b-3c9e1dd0a0f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532669 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+26\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${4}^{2},$ writing the constant term $26$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 4y+{4}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}-{4}^{2}+26\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-4)^{2}+10.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532597 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1069 "q-141532" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-8x+16)-2$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "924bb0ed-916c-53af-b20c-31d4530221fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532680 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x+16 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-8x+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4x+{4}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-4)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-8x+16)-2 & = \\\\ (x-4)^{2}-2 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-12y+15.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93dc778c-0905-5be0-ac96-c8581ce3887e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532681 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+15\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $15$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+15\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-6)^{2}-21.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532539 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532601 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1071 "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}+20y+106.$\n\n${y}^{2}+20y+106=(${{field-1}}$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b855896c-0e74-5e62-9ab7-478fd01cda70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532698 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+106\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${10}^{2},$ writing the constant term $106$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 10y+{10}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{10}^{2}+106\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}-{10}^{2}+106\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+10)^{2}+6.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532514 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532542 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250394" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1071 "Rewrite the following expression as the sum of a perfect square and a constant.\n\n$({x}^{2}+18x+81)+5=(${{field-1}}$)^{2}\\,+$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ba6abf23-a5c2-510c-be10-8fd79a136d85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532699 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2ax+{a}^{2}=(x+a)^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+18x+81 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}+2\\cdot 9x+{9}^{2} & = \\\\ (x+9)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+18x+81)+5 & = \\\\ (x+9)^{2}+5 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
